<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/ohio-farm.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/ohio-farm-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/ohio-farm.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/service-cities/">Service cities</a></li><li>Lima</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Northwest Ohio · Allen County</p><h1>Lima Insurance Appraiser for Allen County, Ohio</h1><p class="lede">On the flat land around Lima, storms arrive with little to slow them. As an insurance appraiser in Lima, Ohio, we value homes, garages and farm outbuildings with equal care.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Allen County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Northwest Ohio</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="snap"><div><small>County</small><b>Allen County</b></div><div><small>Region</small><b>Northwest Ohio</b></div><div class="wide"><small>Housing mix</small><span>Lima pairs early-1900s brick and frame houses in the city with ranch homes in the surrounding townships and farmsteads that include barns, pole buildings and detached garages.</span></div><div class="wide"><small>Storm history</small><span>Open Northwest Ohio farmland lets thunderstorm winds and hail arrive at full strength, and the March 14, 2024 Indian Lake EF3 tornado struck nearby Logan County.</span></div><div class="wide"><small>Local claim risks</small><ul class="chips"><li>Straight-line wind</li><li>Hail</li><li>Tornadoes</li><li>Outbuilding damage</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul></div></div>
<h2>Wide-open exposure</h2>
<p>Lima sits in Allen County in the flat farmland of Northwest Ohio, part of the old Great Black Swamp region. With few hills or forests to break the wind, thunderstorm gusts and hail hit homes and outbuildings at full strength. Summer squall lines, spring hail and the occasional tornado are the main threats. The March 14, 2024 Indian Lake EF3 tornado in neighboring Logan County was a recent reminder of how quickly that can change, and the September 2008 remnants of Hurricane Ike brought hurricane-force gusts across the region.</p>
<h2>City homes, township homes and farmsteads</h2>
<p>Lima’s older neighborhoods hold early-1900s brick and frame homes, many with slate or multi-layer roofs and full basements. Shawnee, American and Bath Townships add ranches and split-levels from the mid-century onward. Out toward Elida, Delphos, Spencerville and Cridersville, many properties are working or former farms with barns, pole buildings and detached garages.</p>
<h2>Claim disputes common around Lima</h2>
<ul>
<li>Outbuildings valued at depreciated actual cash value with little explanation.</li>
<li>Metal roofs and siding on pole barns dented by hail but called “cosmetic.”</li>
<li>Wind damage to older city roofs blamed on age and wear.</li>
<li>Other-structures limits too low to cover a barn, garage and fencing together.</li>
<li>Percentage wind/hail deductibles that eat into or erase a moderate claim.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cosmetic-damage exclusions for metal roofing appear on some policies, so check your policy language carefully. If your dispute centers on wind and hail, our <a href="/appraisal-practice/hail-roof-appraisal/">hail and roof appraisal</a> service details how we document dents, punctures and functional damage.</p>
<p>An insurance appraiser in Lima, Ohio often spends as much time on the outbuildings as on the house. Barn roofs, pole-building panels, overhead doors and fencing each need their own measurements and pricing, and missing one can leave a meaningful amount unpaid.</p>
<h2>Our approach in Allen County</h2>
<p>We inspect every damaged structure, measure roofs and walls, and write a detailed estimate priced for Northwest Ohio. We then compare it with the insurer’s and explain where and why the numbers differ. If your policy has an appraisal clause, we can serve as your appraiser and work with the insurer’s appraiser and an umpire if needed. Before you decide, the <a href="/appraisal-workbench/percentage-deductible-calculator/">percentage deductible calculator</a> can show what your deductible means in dollars.</p>
<h3>Farm and commercial properties</h3>
<p>Some farm buildings are covered under a farm or commercial policy rather than a homeowners policy, and the coverage forms differ. We can review either type. Larger or mixed-use properties follow the same appraisal process.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">List every structure</b>After a storm, walk the whole property and photograph every building, fence and outdoor fixture. It is easy to miss a damaged shed or fence line until months later.</div>
<h2>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h2><p>We inspect in and around Lima, including Shawnee Township, American Township, Bath Township, Elida, Delphos, Spencerville, Cridersville, Wapakoneta, and anywhere else in Allen County and the surrounding counties.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services in Lima</h2><ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/hail-roof-appraisal/">Hail &amp; Roof Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/tornado-windstorm-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Windstorm Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/derecho-remnant-storm-appraisal/">Derecho &amp; Hurricane Remnant Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Lima claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are barns and pole buildings around Lima covered under my homeowners policy?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some are covered under the policy’s other-structures coverage, often with a limit tied to the dwelling amount. Working farm buildings may need a farm policy instead. Check your policy to see which structures are listed and how they are valued.</p></div></details><details><summary>My metal pole barn has hail dents. Will the insurer pay?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the policy. Some exclude cosmetic damage to metal roofing and siding, while others pay for dents that affect appearance. If the hail caused punctures or damaged seams and fasteners, that is usually treated as functional damage.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does flat Allen County farmland really change wind risk?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Open terrain allows wind to arrive with little to slow it, so homes and outbuildings can take the full force of thunderstorm gusts. That is one reason wind and hail claims are common in Northwest Ohio.</p></div></details></div>
